  slide "The delta-cycle split: why defer_drive exists" {
    eyebrow: "6 · Delta cycles"
    title: "run()'s atomic drain would commit a freed slot before its external replacement exists"
    lede: "run()'s drain_current_timestamp drains its admission queue inside the same loop pass, so it stays atomic. Steppable's admission arrives externally — a SlotPool-gated driver submits one replacement per observed terminal — so an atomic single-pass drain would COMMIT the freed pipeline stage before that replacement is submitted, landing it one step late relative to run()."
    narration: "This is the single most intimate mechanism in the whole seam. The fix is a delta cycle split, the same VHDL and Ptolemy discrete event technique used to let combinational logic settle within one instant while keeping a strict global order across instants. The deferred step function first checks a pending-drive flag: if set, it clears the flag and drains the current timestamp. Otherwise it evaluates completions and may defer — which yields the terminal event first and defers the commit. The driver observes that terminal, submits its replacement at time T, and only then does the next call to step actually drive the freed pipeline stage — at the same instant T, byte-matching what the native run loop's in-drain admission would have produced."
    term: {
      word: "delta cycle"
      meaning: "A discrete-event simulation technique: within one virtual instant, a component may react more than once as new events settle, forming a fixed point — used here to yield a terminal before committing its freed slot's replacement, so results stay 'repeatable' independent of iteration order."
    }
    points: [
      "evaluate_completions_and_maybe_defer(): sets drive_pending = true and returns early with just the terminal event when a completion needs a deferred commit.",
      "Next step() call: drive_pending is true → clear it → drain_current_timestamp() actually drives the now-submitted replacement.",
      "Holds under KV routing too: the router's admission queue is empty at a completion, so the freed slot is filled by the driver's post-yield submit — admitted on arrival, COMMIT-driven now, exactly like round-robin.",
      "Cited prior art: VHDLwhiz \"Delta cycles explained\"; Ptolemy II Timed SR compositionality analysis — both referenced directly in the source doc comment."
    ]
    caption: "yield terminal at T → driver submits replacement at T → COMMIT drives it at T. Same instant, right order."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(id = "s5-hdr", title = "DELTA-CYCLE DEFERRED DRIVE", caption = "watch the pipeline slot as one instant T unfolds", x = 48.6, y = 43.2, width = 1792.8, height = 118.8, surfaceRole = theme(surface.primary), inkRole = theme(ink.secondary))

      sdk.TimelineAxis(
        id = "s5-axis",
        start = 0, end = 4,
        ticks = [{ at: 0, label: "T-1" }, { at: 1, label: "completion" }, { at: 2, label: "submit" }, { at: 3, label: "COMMIT" }, { at: 4, label: "T+1" }],
        x = 162, y = 280, width = 1620
      )

      sdk.Panel(id = "s5-driver", title = "external driver", detail = "SlotPool-gated — one replacement per terminal", x = 140, y = 660, width = 300, height = 170, surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated), strokeRole = theme(accent.primary))
